From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 00:14:35 +0900
Subject: Keep the implied capture set out of an opaque alias seen in its scope

Inside its scope an opaque alias is known by a refinement of the self type of
the class that owns it, `{ type A[X] = Rhs }`. When Setup transforms that
class's info, it gives a `Rhs` which derives from a capability its implied
capture set, as it does for every such type it meets, so that the alias
becomes `[X] =>> Rhs^{any.rd}` for an opaque alias of `Array` under separation
checking. A type alias's own info drops that set (`type X = C`, not
`type X = C^{any}`), because each use implies it anew; the refinement kept it,
under a type lambda always, and directly unless something else stripped it.

With the set in the alias, every `A[T]^cs` written in the scope acquires the
root when it is dealiased. In particular `boxDeeply` dealiases the type
arguments of an applied type, so a frozen `A[T]^{}` in an argument position,
as in a result type `Box[A[T]^{}]`, was boxed as `Rhs^{any.rd}`: the alias was
seen through and its empty set replaced by a fresh read-only capability.

Whether that happened depended on the order of the run. A reference to the
alias through `this` computes its denotation from the class's info, and the
info it finds is the transformed one only if it is first asked for at the
capture checking phase after the class has been transformed, which selecting
any other member of the companion whose signature mentions the alias does. A
denotation computed earlier keeps the alias as it was written. So the same
signature, read from the same TASTy, was accepted in a run that happened to
look at it first, and rejected in one that did not, such as an incremental
recompilation of one file of a module whose clean build passes.

The refinements of a class's self type are now stripped of the implied set,
also under a type lambda, when the class info is mapped.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: opaqueself
